task: stack-depth-margin
agent: opus-depth
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/stack-depth-margin
worktree: ../okay-wt-stackdepth
spec: specs/codecs.md ("How deep a message may be" — the sentence "256 is far
  under every death measured", which is measured on ONE platform)

why: the operator asked whether the limit could come from a measurement
  instead of a default, and the answer was: the LIMIT must stay a fixed
  number (it is a wire contract, and a decode outcome that depends on
  -Xss cannot be replayed), but the MARGIN should be measured and
  should be a test. Right now the margin is a sentence I wrote after
  measuring on the JVM only — 1 000-5 000 for the projection on a
  default stack, 5 000 for Cbor.read and 20 000 for the strict door
  under sbt's -Xss8m. Scala.js and Native were never measured at all,
  so "far under every death" is an unchecked claim about two of the
  three platforms, and nothing would fail if someone halved -Xss or
  added locals to a parser.

what: measure frames-to-death for each door that recurses on input
  depth, per platform, and turn the margin into a law: the measured
  death depth must stay a stated multiple of Codecs.maxDepth. On the
  JVM, measure on a thread with an EXPLICIT stack size so the number
  is a property of the door rather than of sbt's flags.

CAREFULLY, and these are the three hazards, named before the code:
  - NATIVE: a stack overflow there is a SIGSEGV, not a catchable
    Error, and a dead test process is exactly the false red
    scripts/gate.sh exists to tell apart (native-runner-error). So
    nothing that recurses to death runs on Native in the default gate.
    Measure it once by hand, print-then-try so the last line before
    the crash IS the answer, and record the number in the spec.
  - FLAKE: frames-to-death moves with JIT state, frame size and how
    deep the caller already is. The law asserts a LOOSE multiple and
    prints the measurement; it never pins an exact depth.
  - ORDER: measure first, write the law second. If a platform turns
    out to have a tight margin, that is a finding to report, not a
    number to lower the assertion to.

predict: the JVM has a huge margin and JS is the tight one (its stacks
  are the smallest and a Scala.js frame is not cheap). If JS comes in
  under ~4x, the honest answer is a smaller Codecs.maxDepth, not a
  weaker test.
